Recompiler: cheaper culling of host registers (pass 4)

Where a compile's time goes (Mac, 3,163 blocks of a match, 82 us a block): pass 4 "cull unused host
registers" 29%, pass 8 assembly 26%, pass 3 allocation 15%, pass 6 8%, pass 5 7%, the stubs 6%,
pass 7 4%; the cache flush, the linker, the list entries and the expiry together under 4%. There
are no large memsets, no repeated flushes and no debug bookkeeping to remove: the time is in loops
over the 29 host registers, per instruction.

Three of those loops in pass 4 did work that cannot change their result:
- which host register holds the upper half of the instruction's target was searched for (get_reg,
  29 compares) once per host register; the answer only changes when the loop empties the register
  found, so it is searched again only then;
- the tests on a host register that holds nothing before, at the entry of and after the instruction
  cannot match (-1 is no register, and neither is -1 & 63): such registers, most of the 29, are
  skipped;
- four tests that can only clear a register's bit ran a search even when the bit was already clear.

The generated code is the same: with DebugDynarecWarmConfig(2) the core hashes everything passes 1
to 7 decide for each block (all the assembler goes by); that hash and the hash of the block sizes
are equal before and after this patch for a 17,000-frame match and for a forced four-CPU match, as
are the RDRAM hashes. Mac: 0.074 -> 0.064 ms per block.

